## law
law-energy-conservation = Conservation of energy
law-energy-conservation-statement-novice = Energy never appears from nothing and never vanishes — it only moves from one body to another or changes form. That is why we can account for every bit of it.
law-energy-conservation-statement-school = Heat received minus heat given away equals the change of the body’s internal energy.
law-energy-conservation-statement-advanced = The total energy of an isolated system is constant: energy only changes form and moves between bodies.
law-energy-conservation-statement-expert = First law of thermodynamics for a body with no work done: ΔU = Q_in − Q_out. In the model it holds by construction: every link moves +q and −q.
law-energy-conservation-story-novice = In the 1840s several scientists realised, almost at the same time, that heat is a form of energy. James Joule proved it: a falling weight turned paddles in water, and the water warmed up.
law-energy-conservation-story-school = In 1842 the surgeon Robert Mayer first stated that heat and mechanical work are forms of the same thing. In 1843 James Joule confirmed it: a descending weight turned paddles in water, and the water gained exactly the energy the weight lost. In 1847 Hermann Helmholtz extended the law to all of physics.
law-energy-conservation-story-advanced = Mayer (1842) — equivalence of heat and work, quantitative relation in 1845. Joule (1843) independently measured the mechanical equivalent of heat (paddle-wheel experiment). Ludwig Colding obtained similar results in Denmark. The principle gained general acceptance with Helmholtz’s «On the Conservation of Force» (1847).
law-energy-conservation-limits-school = The law always holds. But if you forget a path by which energy leaks (evaporation, radiation), the balance will not add up.
law-specific-heat = Specific heat capacity
law-specific-heat-statement-novice = Different substances warm up differently: heating water takes far more energy than heating the same mass of metal. This property of a substance is called specific heat capacity.
law-specific-heat-statement-school = Specific heat capacity is how many joules it takes to warm 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. To heat a body you need Q = c·m·Δt.
law-specific-heat-statement-advanced = The heat is proportional to the mass and to the temperature change; the coefficient is the specific heat capacity, a property of the substance.
law-specific-heat-statement-expert = c = δQ/(m·dT). The model treats c as constant (values at ~25 °C); for water this gives < 1 % error over 0–100 °C.
law-specific-heat-story-novice = The Scottish scientist Joseph Black mixed hot and cold substances and noticed that their temperatures changed by different amounts.
law-specific-heat-story-school = In 1760 Joseph Black noticed that when equal masses of different substances at different temperatures are mixed, their temperatures change unequally although they exchange equal heat. That is how the idea of heat capacity appeared. He wrote that mercury has «less capacity for the matter of heat» than water.
law-specific-heat-story-advanced = Black introduced heat capacity in 1760 while analysing Fahrenheit’s mixing experiments, and in 1761 discovered latent heat of melting and boiling. His lectures were published by John Robison after Black’s death.
law-latent-heat = Latent heat: melting and boiling
law-latent-heat-statement-novice = While ice melts or water boils, its temperature does not change: all the heat goes into the change itself — solid to liquid or liquid to vapour.
law-latent-heat-statement-school = Melting a mass m at its melting point takes Q = λ·m (λ — specific latent heat of fusion); boiling it away takes Q = r·m (r — latent heat of vaporisation). Freezing and condensing release the same amount.
law-latent-heat-statement-expert = In the model a body stores enthalpy; on a phase-change plateau the temperature is constant while the solid fraction or the mass changes (vapour leaves with its energy).
law-latent-heat-story-novice = Joseph Black noticed: if you heat melting ice, its temperature does not rise until all the ice has melted.
law-latent-heat-story-school = In 1761 Joseph Black observed that heat added to ice at its melting point, or to water at its boiling point, does not raise the temperature but increases the amount of water or steam. He called this heat «latent».
law-latent-heat-limits-school = The boiling point depends on pressure (water boils at a lower temperature in the mountains); the model assumes normal atmospheric pressure.
law-evaporation = Evaporation
law-evaporation-statement-novice = The fastest molecules keep escaping from a liquid surface — the liquid evaporates and cools. The hotter the liquid, the faster it evaporates.
law-evaporation-statement-school = Evaporation takes heat away: every kilogram of vapour carries the latent heat of vaporisation. That is why hot water in an open pot loses a lot of energy, while a lid — on which vapour condenses and drips back — almost removes this loss.
law-evaporation-statement-advanced = The evaporation rate is proportional to the surface area and to the difference between the saturated vapour density above the liquid and the vapour already in the air. The saturated vapour pressure of water grows steeply with temperature (Buck equation).
law-evaporation-statement-expert = ṁ = h_m·A·(ρ_v,sat(T_s) − φ·ρ_v,sat(T_∞)); h_m = h/(ρ_air·c_p·Le^(2/3)) — the Chilton–Colburn analogy. It is derived for turbulent flow in conduits, so for a still surface it gives an order-of-magnitude estimate.
law-evaporation-story-school = The evaporation rate in the model is estimated from the analogy between heat and mass transfer (the Chilton–Colburn analogy).
law-evaporation-limits-school = An order-of-magnitude estimate: the real rate depends on air movement above the surface.
law-heat-balance = Heat balance
law-heat-balance-statement-novice = Mix hot and cold water and you get warm water — and you can predict its temperature in advance: the heat lost by the hot water is gained by the cold.
law-heat-balance-statement-school = Heat given by the hot bodies equals heat received by the cold ones: Q_out = Q_in.
law-heat-balance-statement-advanced = A consequence of energy conservation for a thermally isolated system: the sum of all heat received and given is zero.
law-heat-balance-story-novice = Mixing hot and cold and carefully measuring the result goes back to Joseph Black in 1760.
law-heat-balance-story-school = The method of mixtures has been the core tool of calorimetry since Joseph Black’s experiments. The equation has no single «discoverer»: it follows from energy conservation.
law-heat-balance-limits-school = Valid only if no heat escapes while mixing. When pouring, the model treats mixing as instantaneous.
law-linear-heat-transfer = Heat flows from hot to cold
law-linear-heat-transfer-statement-novice = Heat always flows from the hotter body to the colder one — never the other way by itself. The bigger the temperature difference, the faster.
law-linear-heat-transfer-statement-school = The heat flow rate is proportional to the temperature difference: P = G·(t₁ − t₂). When the temperatures are equal, the flow stops — thermal equilibrium.
law-linear-heat-transfer-statement-advanced = The common form of Newton’s (convective) and Fourier’s (conductive) laws: flow is proportional to the temperature difference, with thermal conductance G as the coefficient.
law-linear-heat-transfer-story-school = Newton described cooling in 1701, and Fourier built a mathematical theory of heat conduction on this idea in 1807–1822.
law-newton-cooling = Newton's law of cooling
law-newton-cooling-statement-novice = Hot tea cools fast at first and then ever more slowly: the smaller the difference from room temperature, the weaker the heat flow.
law-newton-cooling-statement-school = A body loses heat to the surrounding air faster the larger its surface and the hotter it is compared with the air.
law-newton-cooling-statement-advanced = The rate of heat loss is proportional to the temperature difference between body and surroundings and to the surface area: P = h·A·(t − t_env).
law-newton-cooling-statement-expert = q = h·(T − T∞). Good for forced convection; for natural convection h depends on ΔT (≈ ΔT^¼), so the law is an approximation.
law-newton-cooling-story-novice = This was noticed by Isaac Newton — the same Newton who discovered universal gravitation.
law-newton-cooling-story-school = Isaac Newton published his work on cooling in 1701 — anonymously, in the journal of the Royal Society.
law-newton-cooling-story-advanced = Newton (1701, anonymously, «Scala graduum Caloris») did not state the law in its modern form: he wrote about the rate of temperature change, not heat flow — heat and temperature were not yet distinguished.
law-newton-cooling-limits-school = Accurate when the temperature difference is small and there is no evaporation or strong radiation.
law-fourier-conduction = Fourier's law of conduction
law-fourier-conduction-statement-novice = Heat passes through metal quickly and through wood or air slowly. This property is called thermal conductivity.
law-fourier-conduction-statement-school = Heat passes through a rod faster the thicker and shorter it is and the better its material conducts heat.
law-fourier-conduction-statement-advanced = Heat flow through a material is proportional to cross-section and temperature drop and inversely proportional to thickness: P = k·A·Δt / L.
law-fourier-conduction-statement-expert = q = −k∇T. For a uniform rod at steady state this gives G = kA/L.
law-fourier-conduction-story-school = The French mathematician Joseph Fourier presented a memoir on heat propagation in solids in 1807 and published «The Analytical Theory of Heat» in 1822.
law-fourier-conduction-story-advanced = Memoir «On the Propagation of Heat in Solid Bodies» — 21 December 1807; the book — 1822. Fourier built on Newton’s law of cooling: heat flow between neighbouring particles is proportional to their small temperature difference.
law-power = Power
law-power-statement-novice = Power tells how fast work is done or energy delivered. Running up the same hill is more powerful than walking; a more powerful heater gives more heat in the same time.
law-power-statement-school = Power is the work (or energy) per unit of time: N = A/t, in watts (1 W = 1 J/s). In time t a device of power P delivers E = P·t. An old unit is the horsepower (≈ 736 W).
law-power-history-when = the late 18th century
law-power-story-school = This is the definition of a physical quantity. The Scottish engineer James Watt, to sell his steam engines to those who still turned their mills with horses, compared the engines with horses — and so the «horsepower» appeared.
law-mass-fraction = Mass fraction of a dissolved substance
law-mass-fraction-statement-novice = The mass fraction tells what part of a solution’s mass the dissolved substance makes.
law-mass-fraction-statement-school = w = m(substance) / m(solution) · 100 %; the solution’s mass is the solvent and the dissolved substance together.
law-mass-fraction-story-school = This is the definition of a quantity.
law-solubility = Solubility
law-solubility-statement-novice = Solubility is how much of a substance can dissolve in 100 g of water. For most solids it is greater in hot water.
law-solubility-statement-school = Solubility S is the mass of a substance that dissolves in 100 g of solvent at a given temperature, making a saturated solution. As a saturated solution cools, the excess comes out as crystals: m = (S₁ − S₂)·m(water) / 100 g.
law-heat-of-solution = Heat of solution
law-heat-of-solution-statement-novice = Dissolving, some substances warm the water, others cool it.
law-heat-of-solution-statement-school = The heat of solution is the heat taken in or given off when a mole of a substance dissolves: Q = n·ΔH. Its sign depends on which wins — breaking the crystal (energy taken) or hydrating the ions (energy given).
law-ideal-gas = The ideal gas law
law-ideal-gas-statement-novice = A gas’s pressure, volume and temperature are bound together: a heated gas expands and gets lighter.
law-ideal-gas-statement-school = p·V = (m/M)·R·T, or for the density ρ = p·M/(R·T): at the same pressure a gas is the denser, the heavier its molecules and the colder it is.
law-ideal-gas-history-when = 1834
law-ideal-gas-story-school = The equation was first stated by the French engineer and physicist Émile Clapeyron in 1834.
law-ideal-gas-limits-school = It holds well for most gases under ordinary conditions; worse under high pressure and near liquefying.
